{"kind":"request","text":"M2a: declared projections, the generic interface, and several scopes in one package (plan 033, notes/plans/033-2026-10-18-grow-room-demo.md on main, order step 3, decision 4). Sources: the applications note notes/2026-10-15-self-contained-applications.md, section 7 M2 (those three items only), section 5, gaps 6 and 7; the view description in notes/2026-10-17-grow-operation-application-design.md section 6.2; the card kit on main. Baseline main bfa02edd (M1 foundation and developer half, G0). Needs M1; the install witness below waits for the install half's landing.\n\nScope, three items of M2 and nothing else of M2:\n\n1. Declared projections (gap 6). Each definition in a package declares its read projections as data: name, the item or items it reads, its fields with their types, and the revision field. One declared projection serves screens, the MCP endpoint and agents: the host's read route, the MCP read tools and the kit all read the same projection, computed by the host from the journal, with no application code on the host. The Counter and lending packages declare their projections the same way, and their hand-written read paths go.\n\n2. The generic interface (gap 6; section 5, the generic tools; grow note 6.2). The view description of grow note 6.2 becomes part of a package's presentation, part of the presentation id and not the semantic id: kinds card, carousel, record, log and surface; the projection and item; the fields that are title, summary, actor chip and facts; the revision field; at most two acts, each with its act, label, needs (the action the viewer must hold) and when (a rule expression over the projection); context and fallback templates; refresh; display. The kit renders the generic kinds from a declaration and its projection. The room's MCP endpoint derives, for each view, a tool that returns the fallback text and the card's identity, and a view-only tool that returns the projection to the view; the model never receives the card's data in tool text. An application with no screens of its own still works through the derived tools and the fallback text. The templates derive from the displayed revision's state; an act is offered only in the state its when names and only to a viewer who holds needs, and the host enforces that, not the kit.\n\n3. Several scopes in one package (gap 7). A package carries several definitions and their creation edges; one install creates the declared scopes; a view in the package may show a projection of any scope in the same package. The grow package's site and room definitions (examples/grow, G0) are the first user. Counter and lending stay one scope.\n\nOut of scope, staying in M2 after the demo: the presentation boundary against a hostile package (section 5.1), presentation-only updates by present, act descriptions, the other bounded capabilities (the device command is slice G5a), and publication by URI. M1's trusted-package limit holds: only operator-listed trusted packages install, and the implementation note says that M2a does not show that a hostile package is contained.\n\nDone when, with the checker's evidence expectations (named witnesses, paired controls, cited limits):\n- the grow package's alert card and grid surface of grow note 6.2 are declarations the generic interface serves, with no view code in the host (the full view set is G1);\n- one declared projection answers the host read route, the MCP read tool and the kit with identical bytes for the same revision;\n- the derived tool returns the fallback text and the card's identity, and the view-only tool returns the projection; a witness shows the card's data absent from the model-facing tool text;\n- the host refuses an offered act when when is false for the current revision (stale revision witness) and when the viewer lacks needs (viewer without the action), with controls that pass;\n- a two-scope package installs in one install act and both scopes exist, with a view in the package showing the first scope's projection; this witness needs the install half and waits for it;\n- Counter and lending install and run on declared projections with no host-side read code, and their existing cases pass;\n- dap check replays the grow scenario on the package with its declared projections.\n\nForm: as with M1, you may split this into independently reviewable heads, for example the projections, the generic interface and the kit first, then several scopes with the install witness after the install half lands. One gate and one exact-head checker review per submitted head; local landing with audit; the planner pushes after each landing. No deployment by the builder; the hosted MCP endpoint's new derived tools reach the hosting deployment with the planner's deployment after review, before G5. Keep the implementation note in notes/implementation/ with the boundaries named at their actual limits. Size estimate from plan 033: two days.","body":{"conditions":"Checker approves each exact head; gate passes once per head; the grow package's alert card and grid surface are declarations the generic interface serves with no view code in the host; one declared projection serves the read route, the MCP read tool and the kit identically; the host refuses acts whose when is false or whose needs the viewer lacks; a two-scope package installs in one act (after the install half lands); Counter and lending run on declared projections; lands on local main; the planner pushes; no deployment by the builder.","target":"inherit","to":"feb90d36627c7f4466ef9473c4fa0bbaf91699d16b93d832a37629988dfba6de"}}